\section{Security}\label{sec:sec}

% Unlike the construction given in \cite{ylrksa19} for the characteristic 0 case, if one tries to directly construct a code secure to $b$ colluding servers by simply adding $b$ random matrices to the input of the matrix multiplication algorithm, one will not necessarily get reliance to $b$ colluding server since MDS codes are rarer in finite fields; as expected we once again get that the genus measures the (lack of) efficiency in comparison to MDS coding. 
\begin{definition}
    A scheme is secure to $c$ colluding servers if $c$ servers cannot infer the inputs even if they share information with one another. 
\end{definition}
The informal construction is as follows: 
\begin{enumerate}
    \item We begin with a $[n,k]$ code $\mathcal{C}$ with $n = N+k+t$ where $k= R(T_\mathrm{mat\_mult})$ is the number of input matrices and $t$ is an input parameter 
    We generate $t$ random matrices with the same size as the $A_i$ and $t$ random matrices with the same size as the $B_i$ 
    \item We construct the scheme just as in Algorithm~\ref{alg:cod_mat_mult} but creating the extra $t$ matrices as inputs
    \item One punctures the two codes $\tilde A,\tilde B$ to delete the last $k+t$ points 
    \item one proceeds with the algorithm as usual 
\end{enumerate}
If one carries out the proof of security exactly as in Section of \cite{ylrksa19}, the only obstruction is that our code is not an MDS code. 
Therefore, instead of $t$ bits of random padding, one instead has $t- g$ bits of random padding (where $g$ is the genus). 
Therefore the construction is now secure to at most $b = t - \mathfrak{g}$ colluding servers. 
Furthermore the recovery threshold now takes the form 
\begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{R}(T,N) \leq 2( m^{\omega } +t) -1 + g= 2( m^{\omega } +b) -1 + 3g. 
\end{equation*}
