    \section{Cylindrical grid graph}

    We denote the enumeration of independent sets on an $m\times n$ cylindrical grid graph (with periodic boundary conditions in the horizontal direction) as $C(m,n)$.

    Additionally, we consider the \textit{twisted cylindrical grid graph}: when connecting the horizontal boundaries, we shift them by one unit.
    The number of independent sets on such a graph is denoted $\varGamma(m,n)$.

    The following figures are examples of the independent sets on a cylindrical grid graph and a twisted cylindrical grid graph:

    \[
        \cylgridi~~~~~~~~\twicylgridi
    \]

    The algorithms for cylindrical and twisted cylindrical cases is given in~\cite{my1},
    that is, splicing the tensor network in the same way, and the tensor on the remaining boundaries remains unchanged.

    Following the approach in the previous chapter, we define the corresponding constants $f_{C}(m,n)$ and $f_{\varGamma}(m,n)$ (including the cases of $m=\infty$ or $n=\infty$).
    However, since the boundary conditions now differ in the two directions, the boundary effect coefficients must also be direction-dependent. 
    Because the boundary types in the two directions are different at this time, it is also necessary to distinguish the directions of the boundary effect coefficient.
    Here we use the free energy of the vertically infinite case $f_{C}(m,\infty)$ and  $f_{\varGamma}(m,\infty)$ to define the horizontal (left and right) boundary effect coefficient:

    \begin{align*}
        k_{C1}(m,n)         &\coloneq \frac{ f_C(m,\infty)-f_C }{ \frac{1}{m} }; \\
        k_{\varGamma1}(m,n) &\coloneq \frac{ f_\varGamma(m,\infty)-f_\varGamma }{ \frac{1}{m} }.
    \end{align*}

    For the vertical (upper and lower) boundaries, we define them as:
    \begin{align*}
        k_{C2}(m,\infty) &\coloneq \lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\frac{ f_C(m,n)-f_C(m,\infty) }{ \frac{1}{n} };\\
        k_{C2} &\coloneq \lim_{m\rightarrow\infty}k_{C2}(m,\infty); \\
        k_{\varGamma2}(m,\infty) &\coloneq \lim_{n\rightarrow\infty}\frac{ f_\varGamma(m,n)-f_\varGamma(m,\infty) }{ \frac{1}{n} };\\
        k_{\varGamma2}&\coloneq \lim_{m\rightarrow\infty} k_{\varGamma2}(m,\infty).
    \end{align*}
    Under these definitions, we have:
    \begin{align*}
        f_C(m,n) &= f_C(m,\infty)+k_{C2}(m,\infty)\cdot\frac{1}{n} +o(\frac{1}{n}) \\
                 &=f_C+k_{C1}\cdot\frac{1}{m} +k_{C2}\cdot\frac{1}{n} +o(\frac{1}{\min\{m,n\}})\\
                 &=f_C+k_{C1}\cdot\frac{1}{m} +k_{C2}\cdot\frac{1}{n} +o(\frac{1}{\min\{m,n\}});\\
        f_\varGamma(m,n) &= f_\varGamma(m,\infty)+k_{\varGamma2}(m,\infty)\cdot\frac{1}{n} +o(\frac{1}{n}) \\
                 &=f_\varGamma+k_{\varGamma1}\cdot\frac{1}{m} +k_{\varGamma2}\cdot\frac{1}{n} +o(\frac{1}{\min\{m,n\}})\\
                 &=f_\varGamma+k_{\varGamma1}\cdot\frac{1}{m} +k_{\varGamma2}\cdot\frac{1}{n} +o(\frac{1}{\min\{m,n\}});
    \end{align*}

    Note that the (twisted) cylindrical grid graph of size $m\times n$ can also be assembled by splicing an $m\times n$ grid graph, so, similar to Proposition 2.1., we can define:
    \begin{align*}
        f^-_C(m,n)         &\coloneq \frac{\ln C(m,n)}{(m+1)n};\\
        f^-_\varGamma(m,n) &\coloneq \frac{\ln \varGamma(m,n)}{(m+1)n},
    \end{align*}
    Note that the area definition of the ``${}^-$'' version here is $(m+1)n$ rather than $(m+1)(n+1)$, because there is no splicing in the vertical direction.
    And we have:
    \begin{proposition}
        \begin{align*}
            f_C(m,n) &< f_C <f^-_C(m,n); \\
            \lim_{m,n\rightarrow\infty} f_C(m,n) &=f_C \coloneq \lim_{m,n\rightarrow\infty} f^-_C(m,n).\\
            f_\varGamma(m,n) &< f_\varGamma <f^-_\varGamma(m,n); \\
            \lim_{m,n\rightarrow\infty} f_\varGamma(m,n) &=f_\varGamma = \lim_{m,n\rightarrow\infty} f^-_\varGamma(m,n).
        \end{align*}
    \end{proposition}

    Substituted these into the inequalities in Proposition 2.1., we get:
    \begin{align*}
        f^-(m,n)<f_C(m+1,n) &<f(m+2,n); \\
        f^-(m,n)<f_\varGamma(m+1,n) &<f(m+2,n),
    \end{align*}
    and,
    \begin{corollary}
        \[
            f(\infty,n)=f_C(\infty,n)=f_{\varGamma}(\infty,n).
        \]
    \end{corollary}
    This can also be considered that when the width is infinite, the grid graphs on the three faces are equivalent.

    These easily lead to:
    \begin{corollary}
        \[
            f =f_C=f_{\varGamma}.
        \]
    \end{corollary}

